--- A variable that is ever assigned a float is a float (spec §1), and
--- so is what holds one: a list or a map of ints handed a float holds
--- floats from then on, a record's float field given an int that can
--- fail holds a float that can fail, and a list of rows made of a local
--- leaves the local to be read again. (Compiled, none of the three
--- built; interpreted, `push[[1, 2], 2.5]` kept an int among floats.)
use math, frame

xs = push[[1, 2], 2.5]
log[xs]
log[type[xs[0]]]
ys = push[range[3], 2.5]
log[ys]
log[type[ys[0]]]
log[concat[range[2], ys]]
log[contains[range[3], 2.0]]
log[contains[range[3], 2.5]]
m = set[map[a = 1], 'b', 2.5]
log[m]
log[type[m['a']]]
log[concat[[1, 2], [0.5]]]
log[type[concat[[0.5], [1, 2]][2]]]

struct P [x, y]
a = P[1.5, 2.0]
df = with[frame.new[], 'a', [1, 2, 3]]
b = P[len[frame.stack[df, df]], 3.0]
log[b.x]
log[type[b.x]]
c = P[int['12'], 3.0]
log[c.x]
log[type[c.x]]
d = P[int['x'], 3.0]
log[d.x.ok]

v = ones[3]
w = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
log[math.stack[[v, w]]]
for x in v
    log[x * 2.0]
